WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.196
Observation data should remain connected to mission context.

00:00:04.196 --> 00:00:11.484
Use consistent file names that include a date, mission identifier, platform or sensor, and sequence.

00:00:11.484 --> 00:00:16.121
Avoid generic names that lose meaning after files move.

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:25.617
Preserve available metadata such as capture time, location, altitude estimate, orientation, device, and camera settings when the mission requires it.

00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:35.113
Metadata is useful context but can be incomplete, altered, or imprecise, so it should not be treated as absolute proof.

00:00:35.113 --> 00:00:43.726
Flight logs and source notes should record objective, crew, equipment, route, weather, lighting, settings, deviations, and significant events.

00:00:43.726 --> 00:00:51.456
Map references link images to the area of interest and help reviewers understand coverage and gaps.

00:00:51.456 --> 00:00:54.768
Protect original imagery from accidental editing.

00:00:54.768 --> 00:01:00.731
Store working copies, annotations, crops, and processed products separately, and document transformations.

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:07.577
Every observation in a report should point to the relevant original or controlled derivative.

00:01:07.577 --> 00:01:10.889
Storage and access should reflect sensitivity.

00:01:10.889 --> 00:01:15.969
Imagery may reveal private property, people, infrastructure, or operational details.

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:22.815
Use approved encrypted storage and transfer where appropriate, restrict access, and log significant exports.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:27.452
Retention should match mission, policy, legal, and evidentiary needs.

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:32.973
Remove unnecessary data through approved processes when the retention period ends.

00:01:32.973 --> 00:01:40.261
Good documentation makes analysis repeatable without turning a limited mission into an indefinite surveillance archive.
